    "message": "feat(admin): every binary setting is three-state — true, false, or not set\n\nA checkbox has two positions. A settings file has three states: the key says\ntrue, the key says false, or the key is absent and Claude Code applies its own\ndefault. Unchecking a box wrote `false`, which is a different instruction from\n\"I have no opinion\" — and for a setting whose default is true, writing false\nactively changes behaviour the user only meant to stop overriding. There was no\nway to say \"not set\" at all, so once a boolean was written it could never be\ntaken back out from this page.\n\nBooleans now render as a segmented control with every state visible at once and\nreachable in one click:\n\n  settings key   True | False | Not set\n  env variable   On   | Off   | Not set     (writes \"1\" / \"0\" / removes)\n\nThe presence-read family is the exception, with a reason. Claude Code reads\nonly whether DISABLE_TELEMETRY and its five siblings exist, so \"false\" is not a\nstate they can be in; those get On | Not set, and the row's `presence` badge\nalready explains why. FORCE_HYPERLINK keeps all three, since it is parsed as a\nnumber and 0 genuinely is off.\n\n\"Not set\" carries a tooltip naming the default it hands back to, so choosing it\nis an informed choice rather than a guess about what happens next.\n\nAccessibility, and a deliberate break with convention: the selected segment is\na raised neutral surface with an accent underline, not accent-coloured text.\nAccent text is what the rest of this dashboard uses for a selected state, but\nat 12px on the light theme's raised surface it measures 3.4:1, under the 4.5:1\nfloor. --text-strong on --panel-strong measures 16.9-19.8:1 across all four\nthemes, and the underline carries the same meaning at any contrast. The\nsegments are a real radiogroup with the row's setting name as its accessible\nlabel, so the control is keyboard-navigable and announced.\n\nThe accent marker is a border rather than an inset shadow because the High\nContrast theme sets --shadow-sm to none, and `box-shadow: none, inset ...` is\nan invalid list — the whole declaration was dropped and that theme lost both\nthe marker and the raised surface. Caught by measuring all four themes.\n\nVerification, real browser against a live server on a throwaway home (8179):\n- Six seeded cases read back correctly: a settings boolean true, one explicitly\n  false, one absent, a presence-read variable, an env \"0\", and FORCE_HYPERLINK.\n  `false` and absent are visibly different states, which was the point.\n- Wrote all five transitions in one apply. The diff read `true -> removed`,\n  `false -> true`, `(not set) -> false`, `1 -> removed`, `0 -> 1`, and the file\n  on disk matched exactly: verbose gone, autoCompactEnabled explicitly false,\n  DISABLE_TELEMETRY gone, DEBUG \"1\", FORCE_HYPERLINK untouched.\n- Contrast measured in all four themes: 18.41, 16.85, 19.80, 17.20.\n- Segment heights identical selected and unselected, so selecting shifts\n  nothing.\n- Two of my own measurements were wrong before the code was: reading computed\n  style right after a theme switch reads through an in-flight 150ms transition\n  and returns the previous theme's colour, and a height comparison that ran\n  Math.round over a \"32px\" string compared NaN to NaN. Both re-measured with\n  transitions disabled and with real numbers.\n- 5 new tests pinning that `false` and unset are different plans, that an env\n  boolean writes \"0\" rather than being removed, and that a presence-read\n  variable still cannot be set false. Full suite 3808 passed, same 14\n  environment-baseline failures as an untouched worktree.\n\nDead .cc-switch CSS removed with the checkbox it styled.\n\nMINOR bump 5.11.0 -> 5.12.0: being able to unset a boolean is new capability.",
